              "printed_page": 397,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "For decompensated patients, see Diabetes Mellitus With Ketoacidosis.",
              "pdf_page": 479,
              "printed_page": 397,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "NURSING CARE": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "NURSING CARE",
          "canonical_heading": "NURSING CARE",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Fluid therapy—in decompensated patients.",
              "pdf_page": 479,
              "printed_page": 397,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "ACTIVITY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "ACTIVITY",
          "canonical_heading": "ACTIVITY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Strenuous activity may lower insulin requirements; consistent daily activity level is helpful.",
              "pdf_page": 479,
              "printed_page": 397,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "DIET": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIET",
          "canonical_heading": "DIET",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Ultra-low-carbohydrate (<12% metabolizable energy) and high protein (>40% metabolizable energy) canned diets may improve glycemic control, and increase the likelihood of diabetic remission in newly diagnosed diabetic cats.",
              "pdf_page": 479,
              "printed_page": 397,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "CLIENT EDUCATION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CLIENT EDUCATION",
          "canonical_heading": "CLIENT EDUCATION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Discuss maintaining a consistent daily feeding and medication schedule, home glucose monitoring, signs of hypoglycemia and what to do, and when to seek veterinary assistance.",
              "pdf_page": 479,
              "printed_page": 397,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Clients are encouraged to chart pertinent daily information about the pet, such as any home-obtained glucose readings or patterns of exhibited clinical signs (e.g., PU/PD or appetite).",
              "pdf_page": 479,
              "printed_page": 397,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "SURGICAL CONSIDERATIONS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SURGICAL CONSIDERATIONS",
          "canonical_heading": "SURGICAL CONSIDERATIONS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Intact females should have ovariohysterectomy when stable; progesterone secreted during diestrus makes management of DM difficult.",
              "pdf_page": 479,
              "printed_page": 397,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
