          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DRUG(S) OF CHOICE",
          "canonical_heading": "DRUG(S) OF CHOICE",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Insulin is treatment of choice and should be initiated at 1–2 units per cat SC q12h; based on routine monitoring, some cats may eventually be reduced to once-daily dosing.",
              "pdf_page": 479,
              "printed_page": 397,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Two U-40 insulin formulations are FDAapproved for use in cats—protamine zinc (PZI) and porcine zinc lente insulin suspension.",
              "pdf_page": 479,
              "printed_page": 397,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Most consensus recommendations support the use of PZI or glargine (U-100) as first-choice insulin therapy for cats.",
              "pdf_page": 480,
              "printed_page": 398,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "CONTRAINDICATIONS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "CONTRAINDICATIONS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "PRECAUTIONS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PRECAUTIONS",
          "canonical_heading": "PRECAUTIONS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Glucocorticoids, megestrol acetate, and progesterone cause insulin resistance. If steroid therapy is necessary, use oral methylprednisolone. Avoid injectable steroids.",
              "pdf_page": 480,
              "printed_page": 398,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS",
          "canonical_heading": "POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Drugs that may increase insulin sensitivity— angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, sulfonamides, tetracycline, beta blockers, monoamine oxidase inhibitors, salicylates.",
              "pdf_page": 480,
              "printed_page": 398,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Drugs that increase insulin resistance— glucocorticoids, estrogen supplements, furosemide, thiazide diuretics, and calcium channel blockers.",
              "pdf_page": 480,
              "printed_page": 398,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Always consult a new medication’s product insert.",
              "pdf_page": 480,
              "printed_page": 398,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "CONTRAINDICATIONS/POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "CONTRAINDICATIONS/POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "ALTERNATIVE DRUG(S)": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "ALTERNATIVE DRUG(S)",
          "canonical_heading": "ALTERNATIVE DRUG(S)",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Oral sulfonylureas (e.g., glipizide)—only considered when insulin therapy is not possible (e.g., owner considering euthanasia instead of injections). Initial does of 2.5 mg PO q12h can be used and monitored similar to insulin. If DM is not controlled after 2 weeks, dose of 5 mg PO q12h may be tried; however, response is seen in <40% of cats and often not sustained long term. Potential side effects are hypoglycemia, hepatic enzyme alterations, icterus, and vomiting.",
              "pdf_page": 480,
              "printed_page": 398,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Acarbose—an alpha-glucosidase inhibitor used to limit intestinal glucose absorption. Often used in combination with diet and insulin at a starting dose of 12.5 mg PO q12h; diarrhea is most common side effect.",
              "pdf_page": 480,
              "printed_page": 398,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Drugs warranting further study for utility in managing feline DM include once-weekly injected glucagon-like peptide 1 analogues and oral renal tubular transporter inhibitors.",
              "pdf_page": 480,
              "printed_page": 398,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
